          <h2>A city centre with its own rules</h2>
          <p>Birmingham's centre is dense and largely ringed by the A4540 Middleway, with streets such as New Street and Corporation Street carrying most of the foot and bus traffic through the middle of it. If your car stops in or near the centre, the practical problem is often less about the fault itself and more about where you can safely wait and how a recovery vehicle can reach you — narrow one-way streets, bus lanes and restricted turns all come into play. A road name plus a landmark such as the Mailbox, Snow Hill or Five Ways island helps the driver choose the right way round the ring road.</p>

          <h2>The Clean Air Zone</h2>
          <p>Birmingham operates a Clean Air Zone (CAZ) covering the area inside the Middleway ring road. If your vehicle breaks down inside the zone, that's not something you need to worry about in the moment — our priority is getting you and the vehicle out of the way safely — but it's worth mentioning if you're planning where the vehicle should be taken on afterwards, since onward journeys through the zone in a non-compliant vehicle may attract a daily charge.</p>

          <h2>Beyond the centre</h2>
          <p>The A38(M) Aston Expressway carries traffic directly from Spaghetti Junction on the M6 into the city centre at Old Snow Hill, and is one of the more distinctive ways in — a short, elevated dual carriageway with reversible tidal-flow lanes. The M6 itself skirts the city to the north-east via Gravelly Hill and Great Barr. Beyond these main roads, Birmingham is largely a patchwork of housing — Victorian terraces and inter-war suburbs alongside newer developments on the city's edges towards <a href="erdington.html">Erdington</a> and <a href="edgbaston.html">Edgbaston</a> — and we cover all of it, from driveways and side streets to supermarket car parks and multi-storey car parks in town.</p>

          <div class="callout">
            <p><strong>Broken down near the Bullring or on New Street?</strong> Stay well back from moving traffic and buses, keep hazard lights on, and let us know the nearest cross street. Around the Bullring, Digbeth and the Jewellery Quarter, loading space is tight and buses run close by, so tell us which side of the road you're on and whether there's a side street or loading bay nearby.</p>
          </div>

          <h2>Getting to you</h2>
          <p>There's no depot in Birmingham to wait for. When you call, we line up a recovery driver who's already working in the West Midlands, so a breakdown in Kings Heath or Perry Barr is handled by someone nearby rather than sent from across the country. You'll get an honest arrival estimate before anyone sets off, and the price is agreed on the same call.</p>
        </div>

